Warning is not protecting, and the silent case was the bigger one. `main master` does not contain `develop`, so every arm of `gc_protected_branches` that was not a configured answer left a non-main/master trunk pushable — and the ABSENT-line arm, which predates v2.2.0 entirely as the original `main|master` hardcode's own blind spot, did it silently and to every repo that never configured the field rather than only those that took the v2.2.0 template. v2.2.1's placeholder warn was backwards on severity. All three unconfigured arms (absent, placeholder, empty) now resolve the repo's real trunk through one helper from `git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` and UNION it with `main master` — union, never replacement, so a repo with both does not lose main's protection to a fix for develop's; deduped; explicitly not "every local branch", which is the mirror of the original bug; `none` and any explicit value untouched, and the absent arm does not warn because nothing there is misconfigured. Placed in the hook resolver rather than the apply layer because the apply layer is not in this repository, because the exposure is live now and one file copy repairs every synced repo, and because the git call sits inside the fallback path only. Note the deliberate asymmetry: fail CLOSED when the COMMAND is unreadable (an integrity failure), fail OPEN when the REPO'S CONFIGURATION is unreadable (an unconfigured environment) — so an unresolvable `origin/HEAD` lets the push through, but a repo that also has no local main or master warns and names `git remote set-head origin -a`. The proposed push-time three-conjunct block is unreachable once the fallback resolves the trunk and was not shipped as dead code. The prose `- **Branch strategy**:` line drops `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`, which no manifest fills. sync-template gains three fixes from consumer reports: step 2b's backup derived its set with `git check-ignore --stdin`, which on Windows tests `CLAUDE.local.md\r`, matches nothing,